exec(open('work/scripts/research_notes.py').read().split("if __name__=='__main__':")[0])
s='V. Elementary formal structures, inverse functions and integration'
add('N77',s,39,'013','A unit of k need not already be a unit of k0','reading question',r'The parenthesis “donc en fait $\in k_0^*$” after the simple-root derivative condition uses the stronger reflection of units discussed in Corollary 1. Under H alone this step is not automatic: the family in N76 has $k_0=\mathbb Z\subset\mathbb Q=k$, so $2$ is a counterexample to that general inference. The implicit-function construction still applies to a derivative invertible in $k$; its validity should not be confused with validity of the parenthesis.',['HENS'],'adds a qualification to one proof step without contradicting the implicit-function conclusion','inverse-function|henselian|unit')
add('N78',s,39,'012–013','Coprime-factor lifting can be recovered directly from H','own idea',r'Assume full permissible substitution and constant inclusion identify $\mathcal A_n/(\mathcal A_n\cap\mathfrak m_n)$ with $C=\mathcal A_0$, and let a monic $P$ reduce to coprime monic factors $G_0H_0$ in $C[Z]$. Center the coefficient-multiplication map at $(G_0,H_0)$: $(u,v)\mapsto\operatorname{coeff}((G_0+u)(H_0+v)-G_0H_0)$, with $\deg u<\deg G_0$ and $\deg v<\deg H_0$; its Jacobian is the Sylvester map, whose determinant is a unit resultant. H supplies its inverse in the family, and substitution of the zero-constant coefficient vector of $P-G_0H_0$ gives the two monic factors in $\mathcal A_n[Z]$, establishing the henselian-pair conclusion together with N75, without differential closure.',['HENS'],'adds a proof repair supporting 39-inverse-function-axioms-henselian under explicit substitution and constant hypotheses','inverse-function|henselian|factor','high','the centered polynomial map is pointed and its invertible Sylvester matrix gives exactly the inverse-function problem required by H',bib=['P6'])
add('N79',s,39,'013','The implicit-function proof uses legal zero evaluation','unpursued implication',r'For Corollary 3 the map “$(x_1,\ldots,x_n,\zeta)\mapsto(x_1,\ldots,x_n,P(x,\zeta))$” is pointed and has invertible Jacobian by the stated assumptions; its inverse is then evaluated at “$\varphi(t_1,\ldots,t_n,0)$”. This restriction is permitted, in contrast with the evaluation at 1 on scan 012, and supplies the direct argument supporting the implicit-function part of the Hua entry. A shift by a nonnilpotent constant in the polynomial Corollary 2 can be performed as finite coefficient algebra, without claiming that translation by that constant is a pointed formal morphism.',['HENS'],'restates and checks the implicit-function part of the Hua lead; adds the distinction between finite polynomial translation and forbidden formal substitution','inverse-function|implicit|henselian',bib=['P5'])
add('N80',s,39,'013','Faithful flatness is an explicit unresolved question','self-flagged',r'The margin asks “a) b) c) d) impliquent-ils que $S_n$ est fid. plat sur $\mathcal A_n$ (si $k$ … l’est sur $k_0$)?” The changing labels make it essential to determine whether d) here refers to inverse closure or to the differential condition being moved to scan 015; the two readings have different answers even to faithfulness. No proof of flatness for the general family is supplied on these pages.',['HENS','M14'],'adds an open flatness question not settled by the Hua henselian-pair statement','flat|fid|inverse-function|effectivity')
add('N81',s,39,'013, 015','Polynomial families refute faithfulness under the differential-only reading','own idea',r'If d) in the preceding question means only differential closure, take $k=k_0=\mathbb Q$ and $\mathcal A_n=\mathbb Q[t_1,\ldots,t_n]$, which satisfy coordinate, ring, permissible-composition and divided-derivative closure. For $n=1$, the nonzero $\mathcal A_1$-module $\mathcal A_1/(1-t)$ tensors to zero over $\mathbb Q[[t]]$, since $1-t$ is a formal unit, so the inclusion is not faithfully flat. This answers only that interpretation of the question, because the polynomial family does not satisfy formal inverse closure.',['HENS','M14'],'adds a conditional negative answer to the flatness question; does not contradict the Hua claim','flat|inverse-function|effectivity','high','the stated module directly detects failure of faithfulness, and polynomial divided derivatives and compositions remain polynomial')
add('N82',s,39,'015','Divided derivatives carry information lost by ordinary derivatives','own idea',r'In characteristic $p$, an ordinary derivative annihilates $a t^p$, whereas the divided operator $D_p$ extracts $a$; this explains why the coefficient-extraction consequence requires the elementary divided operators rather than only first derivatives. The example is an operator-level test, not a counterexample satisfying all the manuscript’s family axioms.',['HENS'],'adds a characteristic-p reason for the differential hypothesis used in the strong unit repair','differential|inverse-function|coefficient','high','the two operators have explicitly different values on a monomial')
add('N83',s,39,'015','The characteristic-zero margin needs a coefficient-ring qualification','own idea',r'The margin “en car. 0 c’est équivalent à la stabilité par les $\partial/\partial x_i$” is immediate for a family linear over a ring containing $\mathbb Q$, but characteristic zero alone does not allow division by factorials. For example, $B=\mathbb Z[t,t^2/2]\subset\mathbb Q[t]$ is stable under the ordinary derivative, while $D_2(t^2/2)=1/2\notin B$. This tests the isolated operator assertion; it is not claimed to satisfy the full substitution and inverse axioms.',['HENS'],'adds a base-ring qualification relevant to the arbitrary-ring formulation of the Hua entry','differential|inverse-function|ring','high','derivatives of the two generators lie in B, while evaluation at zero shows that B has only integer constants')
add('N84',s,39,'015','Principal parts and differential operators require functorial verification','unproved claim',r'The repeated claims “structures canoniques, fonctorielles” for principal parts, infinitesimal neighborhoods and differential operators are consequences announced without their construction. A proof should verify coordinate independence, preservation under composition, and the stated compatibility with transverse inverse images; the amendment from “étale” to “submersif” enlarges the relative case that must be checked.',['HENS','M14'],'adds the construction obligations behind differential closure and the effectivity analogy','differential|effectivity|inverse-function')
add('N85',s,39,'015','Coefficient descent is a stronger conclusion than inverse closure','unpursued implication',r'The corollary “$\mathcal A_n\subset k_0[[t_1,\ldots,t_n]]$” follows by extracting each coefficient with the divided differential operators and evaluating at zero. The example N76 shows why it cannot be silently deduced from H alone: a zero-constant allowed series can have nonintegral rational coefficients while $k_0=\mathbb Z$. The ensuing functor is only sketched, and its handwritten name remains uncertain in the transcription.',['HENS'],'adds a sharp distinction between differential coefficient descent and the context’s inverse-function condition','coefficient|differential|inverse-function')
add('N86',s,39,'014','Uniqueness of induced substructures uses a chosen transversal chart','unproved claim',r'The phrase “Cette $\mathcal A_*$-structure est alors unique” is justified by choosing $X\to Y^{\prime}$ whose restriction to $Y$ is a formal isomorphism and invoking (e). To make the argument complete, one should verify the existence of the chosen $\mathcal A$ chart and independence of that choice; the graph criterion in the margin is then an additional useful formulation of the same inverse-function mechanism.',['HENS','M14'],'adds a local uniqueness mechanism relevant to formal effectivity','effectivity|inverse-function|formal')
add('N87',s,39,'014','Smooth completions are assigned a structure by an unnamed construction','unproved claim',r'“Une constr. utilisant e) permet d’y mettre une … structure” asserts a functorial $\mathcal A$ structure on the completion of a smooth scheme along a section. The missing construction should supply local coordinates, the transition maps in the chosen family, and compatibility with the stated base change, rather than relying solely on existence of the ambient formal completion.',['HENS','M14'],'adds a precise construction problem to the general effectivity lead','effectivity|formal|inverse-function')
add('N88',s,39,'016','The quotient-structure criterion contains an uncertain source label','reading question',r'After choosing a transversal $Y^{\prime}\subset X$, the criterion is transcribed with “$X^{\prime}\mid X\to Y^{\prime}$” as the alternative reading of its source. The displayed composite starts at $X$, which explains the mathematical expectation, but this does not remove the ink-level doubt; the uniqueness claim also depends on the same chart and inverse-closure argument used for substructures on scan 014.',['HENS','M14'],'adds a local reading question within the quotient construction','effectivity|inverse-function|formal')
add('N89',s,39,'016–017','Effectivity of the formal equivalence relation is an added axiom','conjecture',r'“f) (Condition intégrale) La réciproque est vraie” demands that an $\mathcal A$ equivalence relation produce an $\mathcal A$ quotient. The following “Heuristiquement” and “‘bien connu’” connect it to integrating Pfaff systems in characteristic zero, but do not prove that it follows from the earlier logical or henselian axioms. It adds an effectivity requirement to the category and should be compared with, not identified with, the separate effectivity axioms mentioned by MPL_14_002-044 §107.',['HENS','M14'],'adds a formal quotient effectivity condition; differs in objects and hypotheses from the earlier package’s excerpted axioms','effectivity|axiom|inverse-function')
s='VI. Formal preparation and the final filtered argument'
add('N90',s,39,'019–020','The theorem is stated without a noetherian or local hypothesis','unpursued implication',r'“$A$ un anneau linéairement topologique, séparé et complet” and topological nilpotence of the lower coefficients are the stated assumptions, followed by regularity of $F$ and unique division with a remainder of degree less than $N$. A comparison limited to complete noetherian local rings would miss the scope of this statement; the final proof and its surviving formula ambiguity must be evaluated at the stated generality. This is a comparison with known preparation results, not a claim of novelty.',['S19','HENS'],'adds a preparation result beyond the excerpted Hensel-family claim','preparation|formal|inverse-function|S19',bib=['P7','P8'])
add('N91',s,39,'019','Reduction to discrete quotients needs compatible uniqueness','unproved claim',r'The sentence “on est ramené aussitôt au cas où $A$ est discret” uses the open-ideal inverse system of the complete linearly topological ring. Modulo each open ideal the finitely many lower coefficients are nilpotent and generate a nilpotent ideal; unique divisions over these discrete quotients must be shown compatible before their coefficients and quotients can be passed to the limit. This is the needed bridge from the finite filtration in the rest of the proof to the original topology.',['S19','HENS'],'adds the completion argument omitted from the preparation sketch','preparation|completion|formal|S19')
add('N92',s,39,'019–020','Completing the associated-graded proof with the full unit series','own idea',r'In the discrete case let $I=(a_0,\ldots,a_{N-1})$, which is finitely generated and nilpotent, and write $\dot F=\dot U Z^N$ as on scan 019, retaining the whole unit series $\dot U$. Finite generation of every $I^r$ identifies the associated-graded power-series pieces coefficientwise, and on each such piece the map $(P,Q)\mapsto P+Q\dot U Z^N$, $\deg P<N$, is an isomorphism by separating the first $N$ coefficients and then multiplying by $\dot U^{-1}$. Induction through the finite filtration proves that the original division map is an isomorphism, and the compatible unique divisions of N91 pass this conclusion to the given topology.',['S19','HENS'],'adds an explicit completion of the manuscript’s filtered proof, consistent with the later primary comparison','preparation|formal|S19','high','the filtration is finite and its graded maps have explicit inverses; the assertion about power-series ideals uses their finite generation',bib=['P7','P8'])
add('N93',s,39,'019–020','An alternative finite perturbation formula makes division constructive','own idea',r'Write $F=UZ^N+R$, where $U=a_N+a_{N+1}Z+\cdots$ is a unit and $R$ has degree less than $N$ with coefficients in the nilpotent ideal $I$. The map $\varphi_0(P,Q)=P+QUZ^N$ is explicitly invertible, and $T=\varphi_0^{-1}\circ((P,Q)\mapsto QR)$ raises the $I$-filtration, so the division map $\varphi_0(1+T)$ has inverse $(\sum_{j=0}^{e-1}(-T)^j)\varphi_0^{-1}$ when $I^e=0$. Applying this in the discrete quotients gives a constructive version of the preceding proof with no assumption that the unit series $U$ is scalar.',['S19','HENS'],'adds an explicit algorithm for the filtered argument rather than a new preparation theorem','preparation|formal|S19','high','nilpotence makes the displayed inverse a finite geometric sum on the coefficient module')
add('N94',s,39,'020; comparison 019','The final scalar alpha does not match the earlier general unit series','reading question',r'The live final line is read as “$\dot F=\alpha Z^N$”, with the coefficient of $\alpha$ and its intervening sign both uncertain, whereas scan 019 has the unambiguous general form “$\dot F=\dot U Z^N$”. A scalar $\alpha\in A_0^*$ need not account for the higher coefficients of the original $F$, so the argument should either retain the unit series as in N92 or explicitly restrict to the polynomial case. This is a post-freeze mathematical reading question, not a correction to the written symbols.',['S19','HENS'],'adds an internal proof/formula discrepancy not addressed by the context','preparation|formal|S19')
add('N95',s,39,'019–020','Cancelled proof routes do not cancel the final theorem','withdrawn in the same text',r'The Nakayama/regularity discussion on scan 019 and the monic-polynomial case on scan 020 are crossed out, while the final associated-graded argument and “cqfd.” remain live. Their withdrawal records a change of proof route, not a written retraction of the theorem; nor does the cancellation by itself establish that the nilpotent-ideal Nakayama argument was false. The surviving argument is the one to assess with the qualifications in N91–N94.',['S19','HENS'],'adds the distinction between withdrawn proofs and the surviving preparation claim','preparation|formal|S19')
add('N96',s,39,'019–020','Unique division yields a normalized preparation factorization','unpursued implication',r'Although the heading says “Th. de préparation”, the stated conclusion is unique division and a free quotient basis. Once that conclusion is established, division of $Z^N$ by $F$ yields a monic polynomial $P$ of degree $N$ and a unit quotient, hence $F=VP$ with $V$ a unit; uniqueness is checked through the same division map and reduction modulo the lower-coefficient ideal. This records the usual preparation consequence that is not separately stated on these two pages.',['S19','HENS'],'adds the factorization consequence while relating the result to known primary formulations','preparation|formal|S19',bib=['P7','P8','P9'])
s='VII. Specific comparisons with print'
add('N97',s,36,'073–079, 084','The arithmetic inclusion has a direct SGA 7 comparison','unpursued implication',r'The manuscript’s “démonstration arithmétique” and “(partiel!)” have a direct published comparison in SGA 7 I, IX, Remark 2.6(a), which describes reduction to a finite residue field and Frobenius weights for the orthogonality inclusion while distinguishing the full equality of Theorem 2.4. This identifies a closely matching method in print, but does not certify every descent step or reproduce the entire adic-base theorem on scan 074. The component-group obstruction on scan 084 has a separate comparison in VIII, Theorem 7.1(b), whose extension criterion is distinct from perfectness.',['ORTH','H9','S19'],'adds a verified print comparison to the unsearched orthogonality lead, with explicit limits','orthogonal|H9|SGA',bib=['P2','P3'])
